fix manifest: `shasum -c SHA256SUMS` now passes on a clean clone

`shasum -c SHA256SUMS` failed 36/36 on the committed tree. Two independent
causes, both fixed here; no proof file was touched (the 35 `OAI/` blobs are
byte-identical to openai/math @ adc7f124, verified per file against the
upstream git objects).

1. The manifests themselves were committed with CRLF line endings, so `shasum`
   parsed every filename with a trailing `\r` and reported
   "No such file or directory" for all 36 entries. Regenerated as LF.

2. `ComparatorChallenges/SidorenkoCounterexample.lean` was listed but never
   committed (only the namespace-adjusted copy under
   `Verify/SidorenkoCounterexample.lean` is). Replaced with the path that
   actually exists. The upstream challenge file's digest
   (`3f2b1aba…`) is preserved as a provenance note in README.md instead of a
   manifest line that can never verify.

Also fixes the reason the digests were only correct on machines with
`core.autocrlf=true`: committed blobs are LF, but the recorded digests were of
CRLF worktree bytes, so a plain `git clone` (autocrlf=false, the macOS/Linux
default) produced different bytes. New `.gitattributes` (`* text=auto eol=lf`)
pins checkout bytes, and `SHA256SUMS` now records the sha256 of the committed
blob content — the bytes every clone gets, on any platform.

`SHA256SUMS.verify` was a copy of SHA256SUMS mislabeled as "this repo's
additions"; it now holds the one file it claims (Verify/SidorenkoCounterexample.lean).

Verified:
  $ shasum -c SHA256SUMS          -> 35/35 OK, exit 0
  $ shasum -c SHA256SUMS.verify   -> 1/1 OK, exit 0
both in this worktree and in a fresh clone with core.autocrlf=false and =true.
